# The guards: the files that check the agents' work, that run beside a key, or that ship (the owner's call, 2026-10-28).
# A pull request that changes one needs the owner's review (the ruleset `main`: "Require review from Code Owners"), not
# the reviewer app's; after the reviewer's approve, the issue waits in Escalated, and the owner merges it: the workflow
# turns on no auto-merge for these files, since the code owner review let the reviewer app's approve alone through.
# The implementer's app has no Workflows permission, so it cannot push a change under .github/workflows/ at all: that
# refusal is on purpose (agents/README.md "The guards"). The workflow's prose (agents/README.md, implementer.md,
# planner.md) and DECISIONS.md check nothing and run nowhere, so they merge on the reviewer's verdict: the lines with no
# owner below take them out of their folder. A skill's other files check nothing either, so they merge too. A skill's
# SKILL.md stays the owner's: its frontmatter can grant tools or declare hooks in the owner's session. The review reads
# main's copies of its rules, never the branch's, so a pull request cannot change the rules of its own review. A new
# file in agents/ is the owner's until a line here says otherwise. `orchestrator/deploy/` and `.workflow/agent_user.py`
# run only when the owner runs them, so the owner checks them then: the launcher `.workflow/approved_run.py`, the
# owner's, shows what changed since the owner's last yes. AGENTS.md is the owner's only in its section
# "## Autonomous runs" and its `## ` headings: .github/verdict.py holds that (`section_owned`).
# This file is this repository's own: a product that uses the loop names its own guards, the loop's folders among them.
